Predicting time series data in complex systems, such as power and climate forecasting, involves making long-term predictions of multiple time series variables. Current methods, including those using recurrent neural networks, have limitations in dealing with multidimensional time series data and their applications are quite restricted. Our approach adapts graph neural networks to fit multidimensional time series data and demonstrates improved forecasting results in comparison to leading methods for long-term prediction. Our proposed method is a deep learning framework that is particularly effective in complex systems with multiple time series variables and is capable of long-term forecasting up to a year in advance. In our study, we primarily use energy forecasting as an example, but its structure is versatile and can be applied to other domains such as climate forecasting and astronomy. The approach was successfully tested and showed significant improvements in forecasting results on two real-world datasets compared to state-of-the-art methods. The source code for this project is available on GitHub at the following link: https://github.com/asharakeh/AGCN_Forecasting. The datasets can be downloaded from the following link: https://nottingham.ukdataservice.ac.uk.uk/kis/centre/centre_id/1010653.HTM. Dataset IDs: PBIE_Energy and PBIE_Power. The ForestLight dataset can be downloaded from the following link: https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO-YE5K2. The ClimateTimeSeries dataset can be downloaded from the following link: https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO-SJY3U. The K2C dataset can be downloaded from the following link: https://doi.org/10.17605/OSF.IO-SJY4V. The metadata for these datasets is listed in the following table: \begin{table}\small\centering
